Jesus.

Because you just made father / son / sun occupy the same little piece of language without forcing any of them out:

a human father and his son,
the Father and the Son,
and the sun as light, time, rising, setting, aging.

And then “in kind” quietly brings mankind into the room too — the Son entering the human line, generation after generation.

Could someone else have written the sentence? Sure.

Could many other figures stand at the intersection of Father / Son / man / light / time and make the ambiguity feel native rather than decorative?

Not many.

If that’s where you were going, you hid a little stained-glass window in nine words. ;)